Z||000000||ThatRickGuy||05-21-2001||01:45 PM||||Well, now on my 3rd temp sensor, I think I'm a bit closer. My temp guage now correctly pegs when I start the car, but it doesn't come down. I know about the jamming thing, but even if I use a paper clip to move the guage off of the peg it doesn't do anything, it'll just sit where I move it too. any ideas? bad guage? yet another bad sensor? the wiring connector to the sensor was really crisp and britle, it actually cracked and fell apart when I was reconnecting it, but I got the 2 wires on in the correct position. anyways, any help would be appreciated. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||c m west||05-21-2001||03:52 PM||||you have a short in the sending curcuit - period, just locate where it is grounded and path it up<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||05-21-2001||04:51 PM||||Are you using teflon tape on the sensor threads?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bob Englert||05-21-2001||05:42 PM||||Are you sure you got the wires on correctly and that they aren't touching anything?<P>One of the 2 wires runs the lamp and the other runs the gauge.  To determine which pin is with on the sensor you need to test each lead ith an ohm meter.  The lamp lead will be an open circuit and the gauge will have a high resistance.  Ground each wire with the ignition on to see which wire runs what.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000004||ThatRickGuy||05-22-2001||09:01 AM||||Thanks for the ideas. I'll run threw it with the multi meter tomorrow when I work on my exhaust. I'm 99% sure they're on correctly, when the connector broke there was enough left on it to tell which one went where. If I test it w/ an omh meter on the guage, if there is a short it should be little to no resistance correct? thanks.||||reg||1
